Z||000009||Oreif||09-28-2000||02:04 AM||||IMSA classes are:<BR>GTU = Under 3.0L<BR>GTO = Over 3.0L<P>The SD4's were 2.7, 3.0, and 3.2 depending on application and class.<BR>GTU/GTO Fiero's were built by Huffaker and the SD4 engines were built by Vanderly Engineering. The ENTAC Fiero was a GTO class with a killer 3.2L SD4. I have pics of each car from a race held at Road America in Elkhart WI from the late 80's.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wade87GT||09-29-2000||11:32 PM||||I got the vintage car racing bug a couple of years ago. About the same time I received a copy of "PONTIAC Performance Plus" (copyright 1985) from FOCOA. Man, I drooled all over that tube-framed Fiero. I was able to track down a genuine, Huffacker-built fastback GTO Fiero. Mr. Huffacker himself told me all about the car and that it was for sale, with spares (eng, trans, body panels, etc.), for $75,000. (that's the sound of my bubble bursting). I'm still buying lottery tickets. FYI to all, those street IMSA body panels are just not the same as the ones used for racing. Notice the wheelwell openings between the two. But if you really like tech stuff, find yourself a copy of "PONTIAC Performance Plus". F-body brakes and bearings, Grand National 20:1 power steering, electrically powered Delco hydralic brake master cylinder, etc.||||reg||7
Z||000011||pHoOl||09-30-2000||07:43 PM||||For what it's worth, i did see a beretta that said GTU on the back of it on the road the otehr day.  Thought maybe the fiero you saw used that beretta's engine, but reading the above description, maybe not.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
